   "container": null,
   "content": [
    {
     "citations": null,
     "text": "{\"attack_techniques\":[{\"id\":\"T1059.001\",\"name\":\"PowerShell\",\"tactics\":[\"Execution\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The alert shows powershell.exe executing a command with IEX (Invoke-Expression) to run downloaded code, directly matching PowerShell abuse for command execution.\"},{\"id\":\"T1105\",\"name\":\"Ingress Tool Transfer\",\"tactics\":[\"Command and Control\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The command uses Net.WebClient to download payload from a remote URL (http://malicious.com/payload), which is a classic ingress tool transfer technique.\"},{\"id\":\"T1027\",\"name\":\"Obfuscated Files or Information\",\"tactics\":[\"Stealth\"],\"why_relevant\":\"The PowerShell command uses -NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden flags and inline command obfuscation to conceal the malicious payload execution.\"}],\"identity_signals\":null,\"key_findings\":\"An internal workstation (WORKSTATION-07) executed a suspicious PowerShell command designed to download and execute code from a malicious remote server (malicious.com) using obfuscation techniques. The command-line payload uses IEX with Net.WebClient to retrieve a payload, indicating a multi-stage attack pattern typical of malware delivery. No evidence of blocked execution or preventive control intervention was detected.\",\"severity\":\"high\",\"severity_rationale\":\"This alert represents activity originating from an internal endpoint attempting command execution and remote code download with no indication that endpoint protection controls stopped the execution. The combination of obfuscated PowerShell, hidden execution (-WindowStyle Hidden), remote code download (T1105), and IEX invocation demonstrates a clear malware delivery pattern. While we lack confirmation of successful payload execution or active C2 communication (which would escalate to critical), the execution itself on an internal host with no blocking evidence meets the threshold for high severity.\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"recommended_response\":\"Immediately isolate WORKSTATION-07 from the network and initiate forensic collection. Verify whether the payload was successfully downloaded and executed by examining network egress logs, process tree analysis, and memory forensics. Determine if the malicious.com domain has any active C2 communication. Review user jsmith's recent activity and account access logs for other suspicious behavior. Scan the endpoint for indicators of compromise and check for persistence mechanisms. If confirmed execution, escalate to critical incident response and conduct hunt for lateral movement attempts.\",\"rounds_used\":0}",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "diagnostics": null,
